        <body>
            <div xml:id="d654">

                <pb facs="0777" n="1611" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1611"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 654.119/198</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Swiss Minister</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Sulzer</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 26, 1918</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of April 24 in which you enumerate the terms under which
                    the Imperial German Government will grant safe conduct to vessels engaged in the
                    transportation of supplies to Switzerland.</p>
                <pb facs="0778" n="1612" xml:id="pg1612"/>
                <p>In reply I have the honor to inform you that the terms for the granting of the
                    safe conduct are acceptable to this Government, provided the United States
                    Government receives through you definite assurances that all submarine
                    commanders have received the necessary instructions from the Imperial German
                    Government guaranteeing safe conduct to these vessels before such vessels sail
                    for Cette. An exception, however, must be made in respect to the former Dutch
                    vessels <hi rendition="#italic">Bellatrix, Alcor</hi>, and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Merak</hi> which, according to a decision already
                    reached, will proceed under proper convoy to an Atlantic port in France there to
                    deliver their cargoes.</p>
                <p>In spite of this decision, the Government of the United States will allow one of
                    these vessels to proceed to Cette under the terms of safe conduct described in
                    your note, provided the assurances mentioned above are received by this
                    Government before this vessel sails.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
